@node Overview
@chapter Overview

Twist is a transfer library and a small command-line client.  The library
has two objects.  A @code{twist_transfer} is the job: a URL, the settings for
it, and then its result.  A @code{twist_runtime} runs transfers, many at once,
on one event loop, and tells the program when each has ended.  Nothing in the
library blocks; the only place the command-line program waits is a latch that
it holds until the transfers it started have ended.  Names, constants and
header files are Twist's own.

The schemes that are implemented are listed under @ref{Protocols}.
Schemes that are not yet implemented have a row in the scheme table, so
a handler can be added without changing the transfer engine.  TLS and QUIC come from
GNU Quickstep.  The reactor does not start a thread of its own.  A
transfer's sockets stay on one loop, and QUIC is one UDP socket on
that same loop.

@node Library
@chapter Library

The library is @file{libtwist}, declared in @file{twist.h}.
@command{pkg-config --cflags --libs twist} gives the flags to build against it.
The command-line program is a client of it and nothing else.

Every function that can fail returns @code{TWIST_OK}, which is 0, or one of the
@code{TWIST_ERR_} codes (@pxref{Error codes}).  Call @code{twist_global_init}
once, before any other function, and @code{twist_global_cleanup} when the process
is finished with the library.

