@node Transfers
@section Transfers

A transfer holds the settings of one URL and then its result.  It is run by
starting it on a runtime (@pxref{Runtime}), and the runtime tells the program
when it has ended.

@deftypefun int twist_global_init (void)
@deftypefunx void twist_global_cleanup (void)
Prepare the library, and release what @code{twist_global_init} prepared.  A
second call to @code{twist_global_init} does nothing.  Do not call it from two
threads at once.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {twist_transfer *} twist_transfer_new (void)
A new handle with the defaults, or @code{NULL} when out of memory.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void twist_transfer_free (twist_transfer *@var{transfer})
Free the transfer.  One that is in a runtime leaves it first, and its
@code{twist_done_callback} is not called.  One that has been reported to its
@code{twist_done_callback} is in no runtime, and may be freed there.  From a
callback, freeing a transfer that is still in a runtime does nothing, because
the call that made the callback may be running on it; cancel it, and free it in
its @code{twist_done_callback}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_transfer_setopt (twist_transfer *@var{transfer}, int @var{opt}, @dots{})
Set one option (@pxref{Options}).  The type of the third argument depends on
the option, and it matters: the arguments are variable, so a number is a
@code{long} unless the option says otherwise, and @code{1} must be written
@code{1L}.  It returns @code{TWIST_ERR_UNSUPPORTED} for an option the library
does not have, and @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} for a value it refuses.

Strings are copied.  The list of a @code{twist_slist} option and the data of
@code{TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDS} are not: the caller keeps them valid until the
transfer is over, and frees them.  A string option given @code{NULL} goes back
to its default.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_transfer_getinfo (twist_transfer *@var{transfer}, int @var{info}, @dots{})
Read a fact about the last transfer into the variable that the third argument
points to.  This is for a transfer that has been reported to its
@code{twist_done_callback}, or that has not been started.  It returns @code{TWIST_ERR_UNSUPPORTED} for an unknown @var{info}.
@end deftypefun

@table @code
@item TWISTINFO_RESPONSE_CODE
A @code{long *}: the status of the last HTTP response, and 0 when there was none.
@item TWISTINFO_EFFECTIVE_URL
A @code{char **}: the URL the transfer ended on, after any redirects, without
the user name and password.  The string belongs to the handle and is good until
the next transfer starts or the handle is freed.
@item TWISTINFO_SIZE_DOWNLOAD
A @code{size_t *}: the bytes of body delivered to the write callback.
@item TWISTINFO_CONTENT_TYPE
A @code{char **}: the @code{Content-Type} of the response, or @code{NULL} when
it had none.  The string is good as long as the effective URL is.
@item TWISTINFO_PRIVATE
A @code{void **}: the pointer set with @code{TWISTOPT_PRIVATE}.  It is how a
program finds its own record of the transfer that a @code{twist_done_callback}
is given.
@end table

@deftypefun {const char *} twist_transfer_error (const twist_transfer *@var{transfer})
Words for the last failure when there are more than the code gives, such as the
reply of a proxy or the reason a redirect was not followed, and @code{NULL}
when there are none.  The string is good until the next transfer starts or the
handle is freed.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {const char *} twist_strerror (int @var{err})
A short English sentence for a code.  It is a constant.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {const char *} twist_version (void)
The version of the library, as the text of @code{TWIST_VERSION_STRING}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {struct twist_slist *} twist_slist_append (struct twist_slist *@var{list}, const char *@var{data})
Add a copy of @var{data} to the end of @var{list}, which may be @code{NULL}
for a new one, and return the list.  When out of memory the list is returned
as it was.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void twist_slist_free_all (struct twist_slist *@var{list})
Free a list and its strings.
@end deftypefun

@node Options
@section Options

These are the options of @code{twist_transfer_setopt}.  The type is that of the
third argument.  ``string'' is a @code{const char *}, and ``long'' is a
@code{long} that is 0 for no and non-zero for yes where it is a switch.

@subheading Callbacks and their data

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_WRITEFUNCTION
A @code{twist_write_callback}: receives the body.  Without one the body is
dropped, not written to standard output.
@item TWISTOPT_WRITEDATA
A @code{void *}, passed to the write callback.
@item TWISTOPT_READFUNCTION
A @code{twist_read_callback}: supplies the data of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp}
upload, the message of @code{smtp}, the messages of a WebSocket, and what a
@code{telnet} session sends (without one that is what is read from
@code{TWISTOPT_TELNET_INPUT_FD}).  An HTTP
request body is given with @code{TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDS}.
@item TWISTOPT_READDATA
A @code{void *}, passed to the read callback.
@item TWISTOPT_HEADERFUNCTION
A @code{twist_write_callback}: receives the status line and then each header
line of an HTTP response, with its line ending, whichever version carried them.
@item TWISTOPT_HEADERDATA
A @code{void *}, passed to the header callback.
@item TWISTOPT_NOPROGRESS
A long.  1, the default, shows no progress meter and calls no
@code{TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION}; 0 does both.
@item TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION
A @code{twist_xferinfo_callback}, called as the transfer goes on when
@code{TWISTOPT_NOPROGRESS} is 0.  @code{NULL} clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_XFERINFODATA
A @code{void *}, passed to the progress callback.
@item TWISTOPT_STDERR
A @code{FILE *} that the progress meter is written to.  @code{NULL}, the
default, is standard error.  Twist does not close it.
@item TWISTOPT_PRIVATE
A @code{void *} that the library keeps for the program and does not look at.
@end table

@subheading Request

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_URL
A string: the URL.  Setting it again replaces it.
@item TWISTOPT_CUSTOMREQUEST
A string: the HTTP method, in place of GET, HEAD or POST.
@item TWISTOPT_HTTPHEADER
A @code{twist_slist *} of request header lines, @samp{Name: value}.  The caller
keeps the list.
@item TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDS
A @code{char *}: the request body.  It is not copied.  Its length is
@code{TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE}, or its length as a C string if that is not set.
The method is POST unless it was set.
@item TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE
A long: the length of @code{TWISTOPT_POSTFIELDS}.  @minus{}1 is the length as a
C string.
@item TWISTOPT_NOBODY
A long: ask for the headers only, with HEAD.
@item TWISTOPT_RANGE
A string, as @option{--range}.
@item TWISTOPT_REFERER
A string: the @code{Referer} header.
@item TWISTOPT_USERAGENT
A string: the @code{User-Agent} header.
@item TWISTOPT_COOKIE
A string: the text of a @code{Cookie} header.  It is not a cookie file.
@item TWISTOPT_HTTP_VERSION
A long, one of @code{TWIST_HTTP_VERSION_NONE} (the default, which lets twist
choose), @code{_1_0}, @code{_1_1}, @code{_2_0}, @code{_2TLS}, @code{_3},
@code{_3ONLY} and @code{_2_PRIOR_KNOWLEDGE}.
@item TWISTOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING
A string: the response codings to accept.  An empty string is @samp{br},
@samp{gzip} and @samp{deflate}, or @samp{gzip} and @samp{deflate} when the
library was built without libbrotli.  @code{NULL}, the default, advertises none
and decodes none.
@item TWISTOPT_CONTENT_ENCODING
A string, @samp{gzip}, @samp{deflate} or @samp{br}: compress the request body.
Any other coding is @code{TWIST_ERR_UNSUPPORTED}, and so is @samp{br} when the
library was built without libbrotli.  An empty string clears it.  @samp{br} is
coded with quality 5 and a window of 4 MiB.  A @samp{br} response is decoded
with a window of at most 16 MiB, the most that Brotli without its large-window
extension can ask for, and a stream that asks for more is
@code{TWIST_ERR_ENCODING}.
@item TWISTOPT_USE_SSL
A long: @code{TWIST_USE_SSL_NONE}, the default, @code{TWIST_USE_SSL_TRY} or
@code{TWIST_USE_SSL_ALL}.  For @code{ftp}, @code{imap}, @code{pop3} and
@code{smtp}, upgrade the connection to TLS: @code{TRY} asks, and goes on in the
clear if the server cannot, and @code{ALL} ends the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_USE_SSL} if it cannot.  The upgrade comes before the login.
@xref{Explicit TLS}.  Other schemes ignore it, and any other value is
@code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL}.
@item TWISTOPT_UNIX_SOCKET
A string: the path of a Unix socket to connect to in place of the host and port
of the URL, for @code{http}, @code{https}, @code{ws} and @code{wss}.  The URL
still gives the @code{Host} header, the request target and the name that TLS
checks.  The host is not looked up and no proxy is used.  HTTP/3 is not possible
over a socket, and another scheme fails with @code{TWIST_ERR_UNSUPPORTED}.  A
path too long for a socket address is @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL}.  @code{NULL} or an
empty string clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_ABSTRACT_UNIX_SOCKET
A string: the same for a socket in the abstract namespace of Linux, named
without the NUL byte that starts it.  It replaces
@code{TWISTOPT_UNIX_SOCKET}, and is replaced by it.
@end table

@subheading Redirects, failure and limits

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION
A long: follow redirects.
@item TWISTOPT_MAXREDIRS
A long: how many to follow, 50 by default.  A redirect after that many ends the
transfer with @code{TWIST_ERR_REDIRECT}, and 0 refuses the first.  A value below
0 is no limit.
@item TWISTOPT_FAILONERROR
A long: an HTTP status of 400 or more ends the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_HTTP}, and the body is not delivered.
@item TWISTOPT_TIMEOUT_MS
A long: the longest the whole transfer may take.  0, the default, is no limit.
HTTP, FTP and TFTP keep it.
@item TWISTOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT_MS
A long: the longest to wait for a connection, 300000 by default.  0 or less
restores the default.
@item TWISTOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE
@itemx TWISTOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE
A @code{uint64_t}: bytes a second for a download and an upload.  0 is no limit.
Only @code{sftp} and @code{scp} keep them.
@item TWISTOPT_INFILESIZE
A @code{long long}: how many bytes the read callback will give, if the
program knows, for the progress meter only.  @minus{}1, the default, is not
known.
@item TWISTOPT_RESUME_FROM
A @code{long long}, for @code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  A download starts at this
byte of the file, and the caller appends to what it has.  An upload skips this
many bytes of its data and appends at this offset of the remote file.
@minus{}1 asks the server how much of the file it has.  0, the default, starts
at the beginning.
@end table

@subheading Credentials

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_USERNAME
@itemx TWISTOPT_PASSWORD
Strings.  The password is wiped from memory when it is replaced or the handle is
freed.
@item TWISTOPT_HTTPAUTH
A long: the HTTP authentication to use, @code{TWISTAUTH_BASIC} (the default),
@code{TWISTAUTH_DIGEST} and @code{TWISTAUTH_BEARER} added together, or
@code{TWISTAUTH_NONE}.
@item TWISTOPT_XOAUTH2_BEARER
A string: an OAuth 2.0 bearer token, for HTTP, and for the protocols with a
SASL @code{XOAUTH2} login.  It is wiped like the password.
@end table

@subheading TLS

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER
A long: check the server's certificate.  1 by default.  For @code{sftp} and
@code{scp}, 0 skips the @file{known_hosts} check.
@item TWISTOPT_CAINFO
A string: a PEM file of certificate authorities.  Without this and
@code{TWISTOPT_CAPATH} the environment's are used (@pxref{Environment}), and then
the system's.
@item TWISTOPT_CAPATH
A string: a directory of PEM files of authorities, as well as
@code{TWISTOPT_CAINFO}.
@item TWISTOPT_SSLCERT
A string: the file name of a PEM client certificate, the leaf first.  An empty
string clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_SSLKEY
A string: the file name of the private key of @code{TWISTOPT_SSLCERT}.  It is
the key of @code{sftp} and @code{scp} too.  An empty string clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_KEYPASSWD
A string: the passphrase of the private key file.  An empty string clears it.
@end table

@subheading Proxies

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_PROXY
A string: a proxy URL.  An empty string is no proxy, even when the environment
names one.  @code{NULL}, the default, is the environment's.
@item TWISTOPT_NOPROXY
A string: the hosts that do not go through a proxy, as @env{no_proxy}.  An
empty string is none.  Without it the environment's is used.
@item TWISTOPT_PROXYUSERPWD
A string, @samp{user:password}, for the proxy when its URL has none.  An empty
string clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_PROXY_CAINFO
A string: a PEM file of the authorities that vouch for an @code{https} proxy.
Without it the proxy is checked against those of the target.
@item TWISTOPT_PROXY_SSL_VERIFYPEER
A long: check the certificate of an @code{https} proxy.  1 by default.  It is
not @code{TWISTOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER}.
@item TWISTOPT_PROXY_TEMPLATE_UDP
A string: the URI Template of a UDP proxy (RFC 9298), for HTTP/3 through it.
@xref{Protocols}.  A template that is not one is refused with
@code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL}.  @code{NULL} or an empty string clears it.
@end table

@subheading Names

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_DNS_ORDER
A string: comma-separated transports, @samp{doq}, @samp{dot}, @samp{doh} and
@samp{udp}, in the order to try.  A list that is not one is
@code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL}.  @code{NULL} or an empty string restores the default.
@item TWISTOPT_DOH_URL
A string: an @code{https} URL of a DNS-over-HTTPS server.  An empty string
clears it.
@item TWISTOPT_DOH_SSL_VERIFYPEER
A long: check the certificate of that server.  1 by default.
@item TWISTOPT_DOQ_URL
A string, @samp{quic://host[:port]}: a DNS-over-QUIC resolver.
@item TWISTOPT_DOT_URL
A string, @samp{tls://host[:port]}: a DNS-over-TLS resolver.
@end table

@subheading Mail

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_MAIL_FROM
A string: the sender of a message sent with @code{smtp}.
@item TWISTOPT_MAIL_RCPT
A @code{twist_slist *} of recipients.  The caller keeps the list.
@end table

@subheading sftp and scp

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS
A string: the @file{known_hosts} file.  Empty or unset is
@file{~/.ssh/known_hosts}.
@item TWISTOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256
A string: the base64 SHA-256 of the host key, as @command{ssh-keygen -l} prints
it, with or without @samp{SHA256:} and the padding.  A key that matches is
trusted, whatever @file{known_hosts} says.  One that does not is refused, even
when @code{TWISTOPT_SSL_VERIFYPEER} is 0.
@item TWISTOPT_SSH_AGENT_SOCKET
A string: the ssh-agent socket.  Unset is the one @env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK} names.
An empty string is no agent.  An explicit private key is used alone.
@item TWISTOPT_DIRLISTONLY
A long: list a directory by name, one to a line, and not in the server's long
form.
@item TWISTOPT_QUOTE
@itemx TWISTOPT_POSTQUOTE
A @code{twist_slist *} of commands.  The quote commands run before the
transfer, and the postquote commands after one that succeeded.  The caller keeps
the lists.  A command that fails ends the transfer with @code{TWIST_ERR_QUOTE},
unless its line starts with @samp{*}.  @xref{SFTP and SCP}.
@item TWISTOPT_CREATE_MISSING_DIRS
A long: an upload to a path whose directories are not there makes them, with
mode 0755, and goes on.
@end table

@subheading telnet

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_TELNETOPTIONS
A @code{twist_slist *} of options for a @code{telnet} session, each written
@samp{NAME=VALUE}, which the caller keeps.  @xref{Telnet}, for the names.  A name
that is not one ends the transfer with @code{TWIST_ERR_UNKNOWN_OPTION}, and an
option with no value, or a bad one, with @code{TWIST_ERR_OPTION_SYNTAX}, in
both cases before there is a connection.  @code{NULL} clears it.  The user name
(@code{TWISTOPT_USERNAME}, or the URL's) is given to the server as the
variable @code{USER}.  Other schemes ignore it.
@item TWISTOPT_TELNET_INPUT_FD
A long: the descriptor that the input of the session is read from, when there
is no @code{TWISTOPT_READFUNCTION}.  It is a terminal, a pipe or a file, it is
the caller's, and the transfer does not close it or make it non-blocking.  One
that can be polled is read only when it is ready, and what is read waits for the
connection when the connection is behind.  The end of the input does not end
the session.  The default is @minus{}1, none, and then nothing is sent: the
library does not read standard input unless it is told to.  The command-line
program names descriptor 0.  Other schemes ignore it.
@end table

@subheading Cookies, HSTS and Alt-Svc

@table @code
@item TWISTOPT_USE_COOKIES
A long.  Non-zero has the transfer keep the cookies that responses set in the
runtime's cookie jar, and send them back (@pxref{Stores}).  A cookie given with
@code{TWISTOPT_COOKIE} is sent in its place.  Only HTTP uses it.
@item TWISTOPT_USE_HSTS
A long.  Non-zero has the transfer upgrade an @code{http} URL to @code{https}
when its host is in the runtime's HSTS list.  Only HTTP uses it.
@item TWISTOPT_USE_ALTSVC
A long.  Non-zero has the transfer keep the alternative services that servers
offer in the runtime's Alt-Svc list, and use them.  Only HTTP uses it.
@item TWISTOPT_PIPEWAIT
A long.  Non-zero, the default, has an HTTP transfer that finds no connection
to share, but a connection to its host that is being made and may carry several
transfers (HTTP/2 or HTTP/3), wait for that one and use it if it does.  If the
server turns out to speak HTTP/1.1, or the connection fails, the transfer goes
on by itself.  0 has the transfer make a connection of its own at once.  Only
HTTP uses it.
@end table

@node Callbacks
@section Callbacks

The callbacks are called from the loop, on the thread that runs it: from
@code{twist_runtime_run_once} for a runtime that has a poller of its own, and
from @code{twist_runtime_on_ready} and @code{twist_runtime_on_timer} for an
embedded one.  The callback of a transfer that has ended is called from the
loop as well, never from the call that started the transfer.

@deftp {Callback type} twist_write_callback
@code{size_t f (char *@var{ptr}, size_t @var{size}, size_t @var{nmemb}, void *@var{userdata})}

Receives a piece of data.  @var{size} is 1 and @var{nmemb} is the length of the
piece, which can be small and is not a fixed size.  It returns how many bytes it
took.  Anything less than @var{nmemb} ends the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED}.  The header callback has the same type, and is called
once for each line.
@end deftp

@deftp {Callback type} twist_read_callback
@code{size_t f (char *@var{ptr}, size_t @var{size}, size_t @var{nmemb}, void *@var{userdata})}

Stores up to @var{size} times @var{nmemb} bytes at @var{ptr}, and returns how many
it stored.  0 is the end of the data.  Returning @code{TWIST_READFUNC_ABORT}
says that the data cannot be read: the transfer fails, where 0 would be taken
as the end and a short file sent as if it were whole.  @code{sftp} and
@code{scp} keep to it.
@end deftp

@deftp {Callback type} twist_xferinfo_callback
@code{int f (void *@var{clientp}, long long @var{dltotal}, long long @var{dlnow}, long long @var{ultotal}, long long @var{ulnow})}

Called as a transfer goes on, if @code{TWISTOPT_NOPROGRESS} is 0.  A total is 0
when it is not known.  It returns 0 to go on and leave the drawing to the
callback, and @code{TWIST_PROGRESSFUNC_CONTINUE} to go on and have the library
draw its own meter as well.  Anything else stops the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED}.
@end deftp

@deftp {Callback type} twist_done_callback
@code{void f (twist_transfer *@var{transfer}, int @var{result}, void *@var{user})}

Called once for a transfer that was started with @code{twist_runtime_start},
when it has ended.  @var{result} is @code{TWIST_OK}, or the code that ended it.
It is called from the loop, after the protocol handler that ended the transfer
has returned, and by then the transfer has let go of its connection, which is
kept for reuse or closed, and is in no runtime.  So the callback can read what
the transfer learned with @code{twist_transfer_getinfo}, change its options and
start it again, start another transfer, or free it.  It is not called for a
transfer that is freed, or whose runtime is freed, before it ends.
@end deftp

@deftp {Callback type} twist_watch_callback
@code{void f (int @var{fd}, int @var{what}, void *@var{user})}

Tells a program that has its own poll loop which descriptors to watch.
@var{what} is @code{TWIST_POLL_IN}, @code{TWIST_POLL_OUT} or both added together,
and @code{TWIST_POLL_REMOVE} when @var{fd} is no longer wanted.  Some of the
descriptors are not connections: the loop has one of its own, which the program
watches like the rest.
@end deftp

@deftp {Callback type} twist_timer_callback
@code{void f (long @var{timeout_ms}, void *@var{user})}

Tells a program that has its own poll loop how long it may wait for the next
deadline of the loop, in milliseconds from now: 0 if it is due, and @minus{}1
if there is none.  It is called when the deadline changes, after a call that
starts or cancels a transfer or runs the loop has done its work.  A deadline
that is due is told every time.  The program arranges to call
@code{twist_runtime_on_timer} when the time is up.
@end deftp

@strong{Calls from a callback.}  From inside a callback a program can start a
transfer, which is how it starts the next one; cancel a transfer; set options;
read information; and free a transfer that has been reported to its
@code{twist_done_callback}.  Calls that run or free what the call that made the
callback is running on are refused.  @code{twist_runtime_run_once},
@code{twist_runtime_on_ready} and @code{twist_runtime_on_timer} return
@code{TWIST_ERR_RECURSIVE}, because the loop is running.
@code{twist_runtime_free} does nothing, and neither does
@code{twist_transfer_free} of a transfer that is still in a runtime.  Do these
after the call returns, or, for a transfer, in its @code{twist_done_callback}.
The two hook callbacks above may not call the runtime at all.

@node Runtime
@section Runtime

A runtime runs many transfers on one event loop, and keeps what they can use
again: the connections that are kept, the answers of DNS lookups, and the
cookie jar and the HSTS and Alt-Svc lists (@pxref{Stores}).  It does not start a
thread, and nothing in it blocks.  A transfer is started, and the loop tells
the program when it has ended; the program does the waiting, or has its own
poll loop do it.

A runtime is made in one of two forms.  One has a poller of its own and is
driven with @code{twist_runtime_run_once}.  The other, an embedded one, has no
poller: it lives in the poll loop of the program, which watches what the runtime
says to, and tells the runtime what is ready.

@deftypefun {twist_runtime *} twist_runtime_new (void)
A runtime with a poller of its own, or @code{NULL} when out of memory.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {twist_runtime *} twist_runtime_new_embedded (twist_watch_callback @var{watch}, twist_timer_callback @var{timer}, void *@var{user})
An embedded runtime, or @code{NULL} when out of memory, or when either callback is
missing.  @var{watch} is called at once with the descriptor that wakes the loop,
and from then on as descriptors come and go.  @var{timer} is called as the
deadline moves.  @var{user} is passed to both.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void twist_runtime_free (twist_runtime *@var{runtime})
Free the runtime.  A transfer that is still in it is dropped, without a call to
its @code{twist_done_callback}, and stays valid to free.  From a callback this
does nothing.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_runtime_setopt (twist_runtime *@var{runtime}, int @var{option}, ...)
Set an option of the runtime.  It returns @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} for an option
that is not known, for a value that is not right for it, and for any option once
a transfer has been started in the runtime.  The options are:

@table @code
@item TWIST_RUNTIMEOPT_MAX_HOST_CONNECTIONS
A long: the most connections the runtime has open, or is making, to one host
(the scheme, host and port, and the proxy), for HTTP transfers.  A transfer that
would make another waits until a connection to the host is closed or is kept
for reuse.  A connection that is kept idle holds no place, and the streams of
an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 connection are not connections.  0, the default, is no
limit.
@end table
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_runtime_start (twist_runtime *@var{runtime}, twist_transfer *@var{transfer}, twist_done_callback @var{done}, void *@var{user})
Start the transfer on the loop.  It returns at once.  @var{done} may be
@code{NULL}; if it is not, it is called when the transfer ends, and that is
the one way to learn how it ended.  A transfer that cannot begin, such as one with
no URL or a scheme that is not implemented, is reported that way too, from the
loop and not from this call.  This returns @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} for a transfer
that is in a runtime already, and @code{TWIST_ERR_NOMEM} when out of memory.  It
may be called from a callback.  A transfer that has been reported can be
started again.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_runtime_cancel (twist_runtime *@var{runtime}, twist_transfer *@var{transfer})
End a transfer early.  Its connection is closed, and its
@code{twist_done_callback} is called, from the loop, with
@code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED}.  It returns @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} for a transfer
that is not in this runtime, or that has ended and been reported.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_runtime_run_once (twist_runtime *@var{runtime}, int @var{timeout_ms})
Wait up to @var{timeout_ms} milliseconds for something to be ready or due, and
do it, once.  A @var{timeout_ms} of @minus{}1 waits for as long as it takes to
have something to do, and 0 does not wait.  The callbacks of transfers that end
are called from here.  A call returns when it has called some, so a program
that is waiting for one gets control back as soon as it has been told.  It
returns @code{TWIST_ERR_RECURSIVE} from a callback, and @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL}
for an embedded runtime.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_runtime_on_ready (twist_runtime *@var{runtime}, int @var{fd}, int @var{events})
@deftypefunx int twist_runtime_on_timer (twist_runtime *@var{runtime})
For an embedded runtime: @var{fd} is ready for @var{events}, as
@code{TWIST_POLL_IN} and @code{TWIST_POLL_OUT}; or the deadline that the timer
callback gave has passed.  The callbacks of transfers that end are called from
here.  Both return @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} for a runtime that has a poller of its
own.
@end deftypefun

This program fetches a URL and writes it to standard output.  It waits for the
transfer by running the loop until the callback has been called.

@example
#include <stdio.h>
#include <twist.h>

static size_t
to_stdout (char *ptr, size_t size, size_t nmemb, void *user)
@{
  return fwrite (ptr, size, nmemb, stdout);
@}

static void
done (twist_transfer *transfer, int result, void *user)
@{
  *(int *) user = result;
  twist_transfer_free (transfer);
@}

int
main (int argc, char **argv)
@{
  twist_runtime *runtime;
  twist_transfer *transfer;
  int status = -1;

  twist_global_init ();
  runtime = twist_runtime_new ();
  transfer = twist_transfer_new ();
  twist_transfer_setopt (transfer, TWISTOPT_URL, argv[1]);
  twist_transfer_setopt (transfer, TWISTOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, to_stdout);
  twist_runtime_start (runtime, transfer, done, &status);
  while (status < 0)
    twist_runtime_run_once (runtime, -1);
  twist_runtime_free (runtime);
  return status;
@}
@end example

A program that has a poll loop of its own makes an embedded runtime instead,
and gives the loop what is ready.  The same transfer, in such a program:

@example
#include <poll.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <twist.h>

static struct pollfd fds[64];
static int nfds;
static long wait_ms = -1;

static void
watch (int fd, int what, void *user)
@{
  int i;

  for (i = 0; i < nfds && fds[i].fd != fd; i++)
    ;
  if (what == TWIST_POLL_REMOVE)
    @{
      if (i < nfds)
        fds[i] = fds[--nfds];
      return;
    @}
  if (i == nfds && nfds < 64)
    nfds++;
  fds[i].fd = fd;
  fds[i].events = (what & TWIST_POLL_IN ? POLLIN : 0)
                  | (what & TWIST_POLL_OUT ? POLLOUT : 0);
@}

static void
timer (long timeout_ms, void *user)
@{
  wait_ms = timeout_ms;
@}

int
main (int argc, char **argv)
@{
  twist_runtime *runtime;
  twist_transfer *transfer;
  int status = -1;

  twist_global_init ();
  runtime = twist_runtime_new_embedded (watch, timer, NULL);
  transfer = twist_transfer_new ();
  twist_transfer_setopt (transfer, TWISTOPT_URL, argv[1]);
  twist_transfer_setopt (transfer, TWISTOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, to_stdout);
  twist_runtime_start (runtime, transfer, done, &status);
  while (status < 0)
    @{
      struct pollfd ready[64];
      int i, n, count;

      n = poll (fds, nfds, wait_ms < 0 ? 1000 : (int) wait_ms);
      count = nfds;
      for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
        ready[i] = fds[i];
      for (i = 0; i < count && n > 0; i++)
        if (ready[i].revents)
          twist_runtime_on_ready (runtime, ready[i].fd,
                                  (ready[i].revents & POLLIN
                                   ? TWIST_POLL_IN : 0)
                                  | (ready[i].revents & POLLOUT
                                     ? TWIST_POLL_OUT : 0));
      if (n == 0 || wait_ms == 0)
        twist_runtime_on_timer (runtime);
    @}
  twist_runtime_free (runtime);
  return status;
@}
@end example

@node Stores
@section Stores

A runtime keeps a cookie jar, an HSTS list and an Alt-Svc list for the
transfers that ask for them with @code{TWISTOPT_USE_COOKIES},
@code{TWISTOPT_USE_HSTS} and @code{TWISTOPT_USE_ALTSVC}.  A transfer that does
not ask neither keeps nor uses them.  The connections that are kept for reuse
and the answers of DNS lookups are the runtime's too, and every transfer in it
uses them.  Each belongs to one runtime and lives as long as it does, so two
runtimes share nothing.

Transfers that are started together to one HTTPS or HTTP/2 host share a
connection if the server speaks HTTP/2 (or HTTP/3), and not otherwise: the first
makes the connection, the others wait to see what it is
(@code{TWISTOPT_PIPEWAIT}), and then each uses it as a stream, or makes a
connection of its own if the server speaks HTTP/1.1 or the connection could not
be made.  A transfer that is waiting and is cancelled stops waiting.

@heading The cookie jar

A transfer that uses the jar keeps the cookies that responses set and sends them
back.  A cookie is sent to the host that set it, or, if it had a @code{Domain},
to that domain and the hosts under it; to the paths under its @code{Path}; and
over @code{https} only if it was marked @code{Secure}.  A @code{Secure} cookie
is not believed when it comes over @code{http}, and cannot replace one that
came over @code{https}.  A @code{Domain} that the host is not under is refused,
and so is one that is an IP address other than the host's own.  A
@code{Domain} that is a top-level domain, or a country code with a generic
second level such as @samp{co.uk} or @samp{com.au}, is refused unless it is
the host's own name, and then the cookie is for that host alone.  There is no
Public Suffix List, so a name such as @samp{github.io} is believed.

A cookie with a control character in it, or more than 4096 bytes of name and
value, is refused.  The jar holds at most 50 cookies for a domain and 3000 in
all, and the oldest go first.  @code{Expires} and @code{Max-Age} are not read:
a cookie lives as long as the runtime does, and @code{Max-Age=0} does not delete
it.  @code{SameSite} and @code{HttpOnly} are not looked at.  The command-line
program keeps no jar.

@heading Threads

A runtime and the transfers in it are for one thread at a time, and nothing in
them is locked.  A program with several threads makes a runtime for each, and
they share nothing.

@node URL functions
@section URL functions

These parse a URL the way a transfer does.

@deftypefun int twist_url_parse (const char *@var{input}, twist_url **@var{out})
Parse @var{input}.  It returns @code{TWIST_OK} and a URL in @var{out}, or
@code{TWIST_ERR_URL} and none.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int twist_url_resolve (const twist_url *@var{base}, const char *@var{ref}, twist_url **@var{out})
Resolve @var{ref} against @var{base} as RFC 3986 section 5.2 says.  A
@var{ref} that is a whole URL replaces @var{base}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void twist_url_free (twist_url *@var{url})
Free a URL from either function.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {const char *} twist_url_scheme (const twist_url *@var{url})
@deftypefunx {const char *} twist_url_host (const twist_url *@var{url})
@deftypefunx int twist_url_port (const twist_url *@var{url})
@deftypefunx {const char *} twist_url_path (const twist_url *@var{url})
@deftypefunx {const char *} twist_url_query (const twist_url *@var{url})
The parts.  The scheme is in lower case.  The host is as it was written, and
an IPv6 address has no brackets; a @code{file} URL has an empty host.  The port
is the scheme's own when the URL names none, and 0 when the scheme has none.
The path starts with a slash, and is @samp{/} when the URL has none.  The query
is without its @samp{?}, and @code{NULL} when there is none.  The user name,
the password and the fragment are not part of this view.  The strings belong to
the URL and are good until it is freed.
@end deftypefun

@node Error codes
@section Error codes

@code{twist_strerror} gives the sentence of a code.  The program turns each code
into the exit status in the last column (@pxref{Invocation}); the status of a
@code{tftp} file that was not found, of a proxy that refused, and of a few others
come from the protocol, and not from the code.

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .07 .1 .53
@headitem Code @tab Value @tab Status @tab Meaning
@item @code{TWIST_OK} @tab 0 @tab 0 @tab No error.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_NOMEM} @tab 1 @tab 27 @tab Out of memory.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_INVAL} @tab 2 @tab 43 @tab A bad argument.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_UNSUPPORTED} @tab 3 @tab 1 @tab The protocol or option is not supported.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_RESOLVE} @tab 4 @tab 6 @tab The host could not be resolved.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_CONNECT} @tab 5 @tab 7 @tab The connection failed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_TIMEOUT} @tab 6 @tab 28 @tab The operation timed out.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_PROTOCOL} @tab 7 @tab 8 @tab The server's reply could not be used.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_TLS} @tab 8 @tab 35 @tab A TLS error.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_CLOSED} @tab 9 @tab 52 @tab The connection closed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_AGAIN} @tab 10 @tab 81 @tab The resource is temporarily unavailable.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_AUTH} @tab 11 @tab 67 @tab Authentication failed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_REDIRECT} @tab 12 @tab 47 @tab Too many redirects.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_URL} @tab 13 @tab 3 @tab The URL is malformed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED} @tab 14 @tab 42 @tab A callback ended the transfer.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_FILE} @tab 15 @tab 37 @tab A file could not be opened.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_HTTP} @tab 16 @tab 22 @tab The server returned an error and @code{TWISTOPT_FAILONERROR} was set.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_ENCODING} @tab 17 @tab 61 @tab A bad content encoding.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_HOSTKEY} @tab 18 @tab 60 @tab The host key was not trusted.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_RANGE} @tab 19 @tab 33 @tab The range cannot be delivered.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_RESUME} @tab 20 @tab 36 @tab The transfer cannot be resumed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_QUOTE} @tab 21 @tab 21 @tab A quote command failed.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_PROXY} @tab 22 @tab 97 @tab The proxy refused the connection.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_RECURSIVE} @tab 23 @tab 93 @tab The loop was run again from inside a callback.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_PARTIAL} @tab 24 @tab 18 @tab The transfer ended before all that was announced had come.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_USE_SSL} @tab 25 @tab 64 @tab TLS was required and the server would not upgrade.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_UNKNOWN_OPTION} @tab 26 @tab 48 @tab A name in a list of options is not one that exists.
@item @code{TWIST_ERR_OPTION_SYNTAX} @tab 27 @tab 49 @tab An option in a list of options is not written as it must be.
@end multitable

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@example
twist [OPTION]... URL...
@end example

The body is written to standard output, or to the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}.  Diagnostics are written to standard
error, or to @option{--stderr}.  @option{--help} and @option{--version}
are written to standard output and exit successfully.

A password, token or passphrase given as an argument (@option{-u},
@option{-U}, @option{--oauth2-bearer} and @option{--pass}) is blanked in the
list of arguments that @command{ps} shows once twist has taken it, as curl's
is.  A cookie given with @option{-b}, and a header given with @option{-H},
are not.

A transfer error has the form @samp{twist: (@var{n}) @var{message}} and
exits @var{n}, a status from the table below.  A bad command line exits
2, with no number in parentheses, and suggests @option{--help}.
@option{-s} hides a transfer diagnostic.  @option{-S} shows it again.
Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer, unless
@option{--fail-early} stops at the first failure, or @option{-Z} is given
(see @emph{Exit status} below).

@table @option
@item -h
@itemx --help
Print a help message on standard output and exit successfully.
@item --version
Print version and licensing information on standard output and exit
successfully.  A @samp{Features:} line names the optional libraries that
twist was built with, @samp{brotli} for libbrotli.
@item -o @var{file}
@itemx --output @var{file}
Write the response body to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -O
@itemx --remote-name
Write the body to the last segment of the URL path.  A last segment of
@samp{-} is a file called @file{-}, not standard output.
@item --output-dir @var{dir}
Directory for a relative @option{-o} or @option{-O} name.  Reset by
@option{--next}.
@item --create-dirs
Create missing parent directories for @option{-o} and @option{-O}, with mode
0750 whatever the umask is.  Reset by @option{--next}.
@item -X @var{method}
@itemx --request @var{method}
Request method.
@item -H @var{line}
@itemx --header @var{line}
Extra request header.  Repeat for each header.
@item -d @var{data}
@itemx --data @var{data}
Request body.  A leading @code{@@} reads a file, and @code{@@-} reads
standard input.  Carriage returns and line feeds are removed.
Repeated @option{-d} values are joined with @code{&}.
@item --data-binary @var{data}
Request body, kept as given, including a file read with @code{@@}.
@item --data-raw @var{data}
Request body.  @code{@@} is not a file.
@item --data-urlencode @var{data}
Form field.  The value is percent-encoded.  @code{name=value} encodes
the value, @code{name@@file} encodes the file, and @code{@@file} encodes
the file with no name.
@item -G
@itemx --get
Append @option{-d} to the query string instead of sending a request
body.
@item -T @var{file}
@itemx --upload-file @var{file}
Upload @var{file} with PUT.  @code{-} reads standard input.  For
@code{sftp} and @code{scp} the file is sent as it is read, so a large file or
a pipe is not held in memory.  For other schemes it is read before the
transfer starts.  A @code{telnet} session is not a PUT: it sends the file as
it is read, and @code{-} is what it reads without @option{-T}, standard input.
@item -t @var{name}=@var{value}
@itemx --telnet-option @var{name}=@var{value}
An option of a @code{telnet} session: @code{TTYPE}, @code{XDISPLOC},
@code{NEW_ENV}, @code{WS} or @code{BINARY}.  Give the option as many times as
needed.  @xref{Telnet}.  Another scheme ignores it.
@item -I
@itemx --head
Fetch headers only.
@item -i
@itemx --include
Write response headers before the body.
@item -D @var{file}
@itemx --dump-header @var{file}
Write response headers to @var{file}.  @code{-} is standard output.
@item -L
@itemx --location
Follow redirections, up to @option{--max-redirs} of them.  A redirect goes
only to @code{http}, @code{https}, @code{ws} or @code{wss}.  One to another
scheme is exit status 1, and one to a URL that names no host, such as
@samp{http://}, is exit status 3.  The @code{Location} is resolved against the
URL as RFC 3986 section 5.2 says.  A 301, 302 or 303 is followed with GET and
no body, and a 307 or 308 keeps the method.  A redirect to another host, or
from @code{https} to @code{http}, does not take the credentials along: the
user name and password, @option{--oauth2-bearer}, @option{-b}, the
@code{Authorization}, @code{Proxy-Authorization} and @code{Cookie} headers
of @option{-H}, and the request body are left out of the request that
follows.
@item --max-redirs @var{n}
Redirect limit.  The default is 50, and @minus{}1 is no limit.  A redirect after
the @var{n}th is exit status 47, and 0 refuses the first.
@item -f
@itemx --fail
Fail the transfer when the HTTP status is 400 or above, and do not
write the body.  Without this option a 404 is a successful transfer:
the status is recorded and the body is written.
@item --fail-early
Stop at the first failed URL.  This applies to every URL on the
command line.  With @option{-Z} the transfers that are under way are ended
too, and each says @samp{Transfer aborted due to critical error in another
transfer}.
@item -Z
@itemx --parallel
Run the transfers together and not one after another, up to
@option{--parallel-max} at a time.  The URLs of every @option{--next} group
are taken in the order they were given, as places come free.  Transfers end
in any order, and bodies that go to standard output are mixed as they arrive,
so give each URL an @option{-o} unless that is wanted.  One line of
progress is drawn for the whole run (@pxref{Progress meter}).  The exit status is that of the most recent failure
(see @emph{Exit status} below).  Standard input goes to one transfer only: a
transfer that would read it, because of @samp{-T -} or because it is a
@code{telnet} URL that is given nothing to send, is refused with
@samp{only one transfer can read standard input with --parallel} when
another has it, and the command exits 2.  @option{--no-parallel} undoes it.
Like @option{-s} and @option{--stderr}, it is not undone by @option{--next}.
@item --parallel-max @var{n}
With @option{-Z}, run at most @var{n} transfers at once.  The default is 50.
More than 300 is 300, and 0 is the default.  A transfer that is not yet
started is not prepared, so a pattern that makes a great many URLs does not
open a great many files.  Global.
@item --parallel-max-host @var{n}
With @option{-Z}, keep at most @var{n} HTTP connections open, or being made,
to one host: the scheme, host and port, and the proxy.  A transfer that would
make another waits until a connection to that host is closed or is kept for
the next transfer.  Transfers that share an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 connection do not
count as connections.  0, the default, is no limit, and more than 65535 is
65535.  Only HTTP, HTTPS and WebSocket transfers are held to it.  Global.
@item --parallel-immediate
With @option{-Z}, start each transfer at once, making a connection of its
own, rather than waiting for a connection to the same host that is being made,
to share it if the server speaks HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.  By default a transfer
waits, so a number of URLs of one host make one connection.
@option{--no-parallel-immediate} undoes it.  Global.
@item --compressed
Ask for a compressed response and decompress it: @code{br}, @code{gzip} and
@code{deflate}, or @code{gzip} and @code{deflate} when twist was built without
libbrotli.  @code{twist --version} says @samp{Features: brotli} when it was.
@item --content-encoding @var{coding}
Compress the request body.  @var{coding} is @code{gzip}, @code{deflate}, or
@code{br} when twist has libbrotli.  The request carries
@code{Content-Encoding}.  Reset by @option{--next}.
@item -k
@itemx --insecure
Do not check the certificate of the server for TLS, including HTTP/3: any
chain is accepted, whoever it names.  A file or directory of authorities that
the environment names and that cannot be read does not matter then.  For
@code{sftp} and @code{scp} it skips the
@file{known_hosts} check (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}); a fingerprint given with
@option{--hostpubsha256} is still checked.
@item -x @var{url}
@itemx --proxy @var{url}
Proxy.  Use @code{http://}, @code{https://} or @code{socks5://}
(@code{socks5h://} is the same), with a port (the default is 8080 for HTTP,
443 for HTTPS and 1080 for SOCKS) and, if the proxy wants them,
@samp{user:password@@} before the host, percent-encoded.  An IPv6 proxy is in
brackets.  @code{socks4://} proxies are refused.  For @code{sftp} and
@code{scp} (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}) the proxy opens a tunnel.  @option{-x ""}
uses no proxy, whatever the environment says.  A proxy is also taken from the
environment (@pxref{Environment}).

An @code{https://} proxy is spoken to in TLS, and its certificate is checked.
The proxy is asked, in that TLS, to @code{CONNECT} to the target: in a stream
of HTTP/2 if it speaks that, and on the connection if it speaks only
HTTP/1.1, as ALPN says.  What the target's protocol sends, including its own
TLS, goes through that tunnel, so the proxy sees where the transfer goes and
how much, but not what.  Credentials go to it as Basic, in the TLS.  This works
for every scheme that can use a proxy except @code{ftp} and @code{ftps}, and an
@code{http://} URL is tunnelled too.  A tunnel has a connection to the proxy of
its own.

HTTP/3 (@option{--http3-only}) goes through an @code{https://} proxy that
speaks HTTP/3 and does @code{CONNECT-UDP} (RFC 9298): QUIC to the proxy, a
@code{CONNECT} on a stream of it that asks for a UDP flow to the target, and the
target's QUIC packets in HTTP Datagrams (RFC 9297) on that stream.  The target's
TLS is still its own.  The proxy looks the target up, so its name is not looked
up here.  A proxy of another kind cannot carry HTTP/3, and then the transfer
fails, and does not go round the proxy.  The proxy's address is looked up, and
its certificate checked, as for TCP.  A failure of the proxy's, such as a 403 or
a 407, is exit status 56.
@item --proxy-template-udp @var{template}
The URI Template (RFC 6570) of a proxy that does @code{CONNECT-UDP}: an
@code{https://} URI with the variables @code{target_host} and
@code{target_port}, as in
@samp{https://proxy.example/masque/@{target_host@}/@{target_port@}/} or
@samp{https://proxy.example/masque@{?target_host,target_port@}}.  Without it
the template of @option{-x https://host:port} is
@samp{https://host:port/.well-known/masque/udp/@{target_host@}/@{target_port@}/}.
With it and no @option{-x}, the template names the proxy for UDP (HTTP/3), and
every other transfer is direct.  An invalid template is a usage error (exit
status 2).  Credentials come from the proxy's URL, the template's authority, or
@option{-U}.
@item --proxy-cacert @var{file}
PEM bundle of the authorities that vouch for an https proxy.  Without it the
proxy is checked against the same authorities as the target:
@option{--cacert}, @option{--capath}, the environment's (@pxref{Environment}),
and the system's.
@item --proxy-insecure
Do not check the certificate of an https proxy.  This is not @option{-k}: that
says nothing about the proxy, and the proxy is checked unless this is given.
@item --noproxy @var{list}
Comma-separated hosts that do not go through a proxy, also when one is
given with @option{-x}.  A name matches itself and the names under it:
@samp{local.com} matches @samp{local.com} and @samp{www.local.com}, and
not @samp{www.notlocal.com}.  An address matches itself, or a network as
@samp{192.168.0.0/16}.  @samp{*} is every host, which is to use no proxy.
This overrides the environment's @env{no_proxy} and @env{NO_PROXY}, and
@option{--noproxy ""} turns them off.
@item --ssl
Upgrade @code{ftp}, @code{imap}, @code{pop3} and @code{smtp} to TLS if the
server offers it, and go on in the clear if it does not.  The login comes after
the upgrade.  @option{--ftp-ssl} is the same.  Reset by @option{--next}.
@xref{Explicit TLS}.
@item --ssl-reqd
The same, and exit status 64 if the server will not.  @option{--ftp-ssl-reqd}
is the same.  Reset by @option{--next}.
@item --unix-socket @var{path}
Connect to the Unix socket @var{path} in place of the host and port of the URL,
for @code{http}, @code{https}, @code{ws} and @code{wss}.  The URL still gives
the @code{Host} header, the request target, and for @code{https} the name that
the certificate must have.  The host is not looked up, a redirect to another
host goes to the same socket, and no proxy is used, whether it is given with
@option{-x} or by the environment.  HTTP/3 is not possible over a socket, and
a URL of another scheme is exit status 1.  A socket that is not there is exit
status 7.
@item --abstract-unix-socket @var{name}
The same for a socket in the abstract namespace of Linux.  @var{name} has no
NUL byte before it.  This and @option{--unix-socket} replace each other.
@item -U @var{user:password}
@itemx --proxy-user @var{user:password}
The user name and password for the proxy, if its URL has none.
@item -A @var{name}
@itemx --user-agent @var{name}
User-Agent header.
@item -u @var{user[:password]}
@itemx --user @var{user[:password]}
User and password.  Without a colon, the password is read from
@file{/dev/tty} with echo disabled, so a piped standard input is left
for @option{-d} or @option{-T}.
@item -e @var{url}
@itemx --referer @var{url}
Referer header.
@item -r @var{range}
@itemx --range @var{range}
Byte range.  For @code{sftp}, @code{scp} and @code{file} it is
@samp{@var{first}-@var{last}}, @samp{@var{first}-}, or @samp{-@var{count}} for
the last @var{count} bytes, one range only (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).  A range
that starts at or past the end of a file is exit status 33.
@item -l
@itemx --list-only
List the names only, one to a line, not the server's long form, when the URL
names a directory.  For @code{sftp} (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item -Q @var{command}
@itemx --quote @var{command}
Run an SFTP command on the server before the transfer, or after a successful
one if @var{command} begins with @samp{-}.  A @samp{*} before it lets the
server refuse it without ending the transfer.  May be given many times.  For
@code{sftp} URLs only; other URLs ignore it (@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --limit-rate @var{speed}
Keep to @var{speed} bytes a second, for downloads and uploads, for
@code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  The speed is a whole number with an optional
suffix: @samp{k}, @samp{m}, @samp{g}, @samp{t} or @samp{p}, in either case,
each 1024 times the one before (@samp{200K}, @samp{3m}).  @samp{0} is no
limit.  Other kinds of URL do not keep to a rate yet, and are refused with
status 2 rather than ignoring the limit.
@item --ftp-create-dirs
Make the directories an @code{sftp} upload needs, with mode 0755.
@item -C @var{offset}
@itemx --continue-at @var{offset}
Resume at byte @var{offset}, for @code{sftp} and @code{scp}.  Use @samp{-} to
find the offset.  A download continues from the end of the file named by
@option{-o} or @option{-O}, which is opened to add to it; with a number the
file is added to from that byte of the remote file.  An upload with
@option{-T} skips that many bytes of the local file and adds to the remote
one at that offset; with @samp{-} the server is asked how big the remote file
is.  Exits 36 if the remote file is shorter than the offset.
@item -b @var{data}
@itemx --cookie @var{data}
Cookie request header.  This is not a cookie jar file.
@item --oauth2-bearer @var{token}
Bearer token.
@item -m @var{seconds}
@itemx --max-time @var{seconds}
Limit for the whole transfer.  Up to three decimal places are
accepted.
@item --connect-timeout @var{seconds}
Limit for making the connection, in seconds; five minutes if it is not given
(or is 0).  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it covers all of it: the name
lookup, the TCP connect, a proxy, and the SSH handshake up to the login
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).  Up to three decimal places are accepted.
@item -s
@itemx --silent
Hide the progress meter and the transfer diagnostic.  The exit status
is unchanged.  @option{--no-silent} undoes it.
@item -S
@itemx --show-error
Show the transfer diagnostic when @option{-s} was also given, in either
order.  @option{--no-show-error} undoes it.
@item -#
@itemx --progress-bar
Show the progress as a bar in place of the meter (@pxref{Progress
meter}).  @option{--no-progress-bar} undoes it.
@item --no-progress-meter
Hide the progress meter and the bar, and leave the diagnostics alone.
@option{--progress-meter} undoes it.
@item --stderr @var{file}
Write diagnostics to @var{file}.  @code{-} keeps standard error.  This
applies to every URL.
@item -w @var{format}
@itemx --write-out @var{format}
Print @var{format} on standard output after the body.
@code{@{http_code@}}, @code{@{response_code@}},
@code{@{size_download@}}, @code{@{url_effective@}},
@code{@{content_type@}}, @code{@{exitcode@}}, and @code{@{errormsg@}}
are replaced.  @code{\n}, @code{\r}, @code{\t}, and @code{%%} are
recognised.  An unknown @code{@{name@}} is empty.  @code{@{url_effective@}} is
the URL the transfer ended on, after any redirect, and has no user name or
password in it, so that a log of it holds no secret.
@item --url @var{url}
A URL to fetch.  It is the same as a URL given without an option, and can be
given as an expansion (@option{--expand-url}).
@item --variable @var{name=text}
@itemx --variable @var{name@@file}
@itemx --variable %@var{name}
Set a variable (@pxref{Variables}).  @samp{name@@file} reads it from a file,
or from standard input for @samp{-}; @samp{%name} takes it from the
environment, and @samp{%name=text} or @samp{%name@@file} says what it is if
the environment does not have it.
@item --expand-@var{option} @var{parameter}
Any option that takes a parameter, with the variables in the parameter
expanded first (@pxref{Variables}): @option{--expand-url},
@option{--expand-data}, @option{--expand-header}, @option{--expand-output}.
@item -g
@itemx --globoff
Do not expand @samp{@{@}} and @samp{[]} in URLs (@pxref{URL globbing}).
@item -q
@itemx --disable
Accepted.  No config file is read.
@item --next
Start a separate transfer for the following URL.  Options after it do
not inherit the previous URL's method, body, headers, output, proxy,
user, or timeouts.  @option{-s}, @option{-S}, @option{-#},
@option{--no-progress-meter}, @option{--stderr},
@option{--fail-early}, and the options of @option{-Z} still apply.  A URL is required before
@option{--next}.  The transfers of the whole command line, whatever
the @option{--next} between them, share the connections that are kept and the
names that were looked up.
@item -E @var{file}
@itemx --cert @var{file}
Client certificate in PEM form, leaf first.  Further certificates in
the file are sent after the leaf.  The matching private key is
@option{--key}, an unencrypted PEM file.  When a server asks for a
client certificate and none was given, the handshake fails.  When one
was given, it is presented.
@item --key @var{file}
Private key for @option{--cert}.  For @code{sftp} and @code{scp} it is the
private key to log in with, and it is used alone: an ssh-agent is not asked
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --pass @var{phrase}
Passphrase for the private key file, for the formats twist can decrypt
(@pxref{SFTP and SCP}).
@item --hostpubsha256 @var{hash}
The SHA-256 of the host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server, in base64
as @command{ssh-keygen -l} prints it, with or without @samp{SHA256:}.  A host
key that matches is trusted, whatever @file{known_hosts} says; one that does
not is refused, even with @option{-k}.
@item --cacert @var{file}
PEM trust bundle used to verify the peer.
@item --capath @var{directory}
A directory of certificate authorities, as PEM files whose names end in
@file{.pem}, @file{.crt} or @file{.cer}, or are the eight-digit hash and
number that OpenSSL's @command{c_rehash} makes.  It is used as well as
@option{--cacert}.  With neither, the environment's (@pxref{Environment}),
and then the system's authorities are trusted.
@item --http1.0
@itemx --http1.1
@itemx --http2
@itemx --http2-prior-knowledge
@itemx --http3
@itemx --http3-only
Select the HTTP version.  HTTP/3 is used when discovered from an HTTPS
record or a cached Alt-Svc entry.  HTTP/2 on HTTPS is ALPN.  Cleartext
HTTP/2 is an h2c upgrade, unless prior knowledge was requested.
@item --dns-order @var{list}
Comma-separated DNS transports.  The default is @code{doq,dot,doh,udp}.
DoQ and DoT are used when the system resolver advertises them, or when
@option{--doq-url} or @option{--dot-url} names a server.  When one of
@option{--doq-url}, @option{--dot-url} or @option{--doh-url} names a
server and this option is not given, only the named servers are asked,
in that order, and plain UDP is not: a server that is down, or that has
been made unreachable, ends the lookup and does not send the name to the
system resolver in the clear.  List @code{udp} to allow it.  UDP retries
over TCP when the answer is truncated.  A host that is an IPv4 or IPv6
address is used as that address.
@item --doh-url @var{url}
Resolve host names through this DNS-over-HTTPS server instead of
@option{--dns-order}.  The URL must use HTTPS.  The server's name is
resolved with DoQ, DoT, or UDP, and not with DoH.  Each query is then an
HTTP POST whose body is one DNS message
(@code{application/dns-message}).  An empty URL
restores the normal resolver.  When @option{--dns-order} is also set,
that list is used and this URL supplies the @code{doh} step.
@item --doh-insecure
Do not verify the DoH server certificate.  This is independent of
@option{-k}.
@item --doq-url @var{url}
DNS-over-QUIC server.  @code{quic://host} or @code{host}, with an
optional port (default 853).  The certificate is checked against that
host.  The host name is resolved with UDP.  An empty URL clears it.
Without this option, DoQ uses the address and name from a
@code{_dns.resolver.arpa} SVCB record (DDR).  The certificate of a resolver
found that way must be valid for the name in the record and also for the
address of the resolver it was found from (RFC 9462 section 4.2), so a record
that sends a lookup elsewhere is refused.
@item --dot-url @var{url}
DNS-over-TLS server.  @code{tls://host} or @code{host}, with an optional
port (default 853).  Same discovery rules as @option{--doq-url}.
@item --mail-from @var{address}
@itemx --mail-rcpt @var{address}
SMTP envelope.  Repeat @option{--mail-rcpt} for each recipient.
@end table

@code{mqtt://host/topic} subscribes to the topic, or publishes
@option{--data} and disconnects.  @code{ldap://host/dn} binds, searches,
and writes the result as LDIF.  @code{ws://host/path} does the HTTP/1
upgrade, or an extended CONNECT on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, and writes payload
bytes.  An HTTP/1 status other than 101 fails.  @code{gopher://host/type}
sends the selector and writes the reply until the server closes.
@code{dict://host/d:word:database} defines a word,
@code{dict://host/m:word:database:strategy} matches one, and
@code{dict://host/show:db} asks the server for its database list.  The
reply is written until the server closes.
@code{tftp://host/file} reads a file.  @option{--data} or @option{-T}
uploads one.  The transfer offers block size 512 and retransmits a
lost packet.  A proxy is not used.  A server error packet exits 68
through 74.

There is no config file, @option{--fail-with-body}, verbose trace,
or cookie jar.

@heading Exit status
The numbers are curl's, so that a script that tests them works with either.

Several URLs leave the status of the last transfer to end.  With
@option{-Z} the transfers end in any order, and the status is that of the
most recent one that failed: a success after it does not clear it.  A
transfer that was ended because another failed (status 42) is the status
only when no other transfer has failed.  A command line that sends
standard input to more than one transfer under @option{-Z} exits 2.

@table @asis
@item 0
The transfer finished.
@item 1
The scheme is not implemented.
@item 2
The command line is wrong, or twist could not initialise.
@item 3
The URL is malformed.
@item 6
The host name could not be resolved.
@item 7
The connection failed.
@item 8
The server's reply could not be used.
@item 18
The transfer ended before all of the data the server announced had come.
@item 21
A @option{--quote} command of an @code{sftp} transfer failed.
@item 22
@option{--fail} and the HTTP status is 400 or above.
@item 23
The output could not be written.
@item 26
A local file named by @option{-d}, @option{--data-binary},
@option{--data-urlencode}, or @option{-T} could not be read.
@item 27
Out of memory.
@item 25
An @code{sftp} or @code{scp} upload failed.
@item 28
The transfer timed out.
@item 33
The range cannot be delivered: it starts beyond the end of the remote file, or
is not one twist understands.
@item 36
The transfer cannot be resumed: the remote file is shorter than the offset, the
local data is, or the file to resume is not there.
@item 35
The TLS handshake failed.
@item 37
A file URL could not be opened.
@item 42
The transfer was aborted.
@item 43
A bad argument was passed to the library.
@item 47
Too many redirects.
@item 48
An option given with @option{-t} is not one of the telnet options.
@item 49
An option given with @option{-t} is not written as @var{name}=@var{value}, or
its value is not right for it.
@item 52
The server closed before sending any data.
@item 56
A proxy refused a @code{CONNECT} or @code{CONNECT-UDP} tunnel, with a
status such as 403 or 407.
@item 61
The content encoding could not be decoded.
@item 64
TLS was asked for with @option{--ssl-reqd} and the server does not offer it or
refuses it.
@item 9
The SFTP server would not let the user read the file.
@item 60
The host key of an @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server is not trusted: it is
not in @file{known_hosts}, it differs from the one there, or it does not
match @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@item 67
Authentication failed.
@item 68
TFTP file not found.
@item 69
TFTP access violation.
@item 70
TFTP disk full.
@item 71
TFTP illegal operation.
@item 72
TFTP unknown transfer.
@item 73
TFTP file already exists.
@item 74
TFTP no such user.
@item 78
The file does not exist on the @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server.
@item 79
The @code{sftp} or @code{scp} server could not do what was asked.
@item 81
The operation could not be started.
@item 93
The loop was run again from inside a callback.  Only a program that uses the
library can cause this.
@item 97
A SOCKS5 proxy refused the connection or could not complete it.
@end table

@node Protocols
@chapter Protocols

A URL scheme selects the protocol.  The scheme with an @samp{s} is the
same protocol inside TLS from the first byte.  @code{ftp}, @code{imap},
@code{pop3} and @code{smtp} can be upgraded to TLS after they start, when
@option{--ssl} or @option{--ssl-reqd} asks; nothing else is.

@table @code
@item dict
Looks up a word with a DICT server: DEFINE, MATCH or SHOW, chosen by
the URL path.  The reply is written until the server closes.
@item file
Reads the local file named by the URL path.  The path is percent-decoded, and
a NUL in it is a malformed URL (exit status 3).  The host is empty,
@samp{localhost} or @samp{127.0.0.1}, and any other is exit status 3.  Only a
regular file is read: a FIFO, a device or a directory is refused with exit
status 37, and is not waited on.  @option{-r} takes part of the file.
@item ftp, ftps
Downloads a file, or lists a directory when the URL path ends in a
slash.  @code{ftps} is TLS from the first byte (port 990); @code{ftp}
is upgraded with @code{AUTH TLS} only when @option{--ssl} or
@option{--ssl-reqd} asks.  On @code{ftps}, and on an upgraded @code{ftp}, the
data connection is protected as well (RFC 4217): after login twist sends @code{PBSZ 0} and @code{PROT P},
runs TLS on each data connection, and checks the server against the host
of the URL as it does the control connection (@option{-k} turns both
off).  The data connection resumes the TLS session of the control
connection when the server gave a ticket for it, since servers commonly
insist on that.  A server that will not protect the data connection ends
the transfer with status 35; the file is never fetched unprotected
instead.
@item gopher, gophers
Sends the selector from the URL and writes the reply until the server
closes.
@item http, https
HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, as the @option{--http*} options
and the server allow.  Over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 the header names are sent in
lower case, and the fields that belong to an HTTP/1 connection are left out of
the request, as RFC 9113 section 8.2.2 asks: @code{Connection},
@code{Keep-Alive}, @code{Proxy-Connection}, @code{Transfer-Encoding},
@code{Upgrade}, and @code{TE} unless its value is @samp{trailers}.  Even so a
header of @option{-H} that is one of them is not an error.
@option{--unix-socket} and @option{--abstract-unix-socket} send the request
over a Unix socket.
@item imap, imaps
Fetches a message from a mailbox, or lists the mailboxes.
@item ldap, ldaps
Binds and searches, and writes the result as LDIF.
@item mqtt, mqtts
Subscribes to the topic in the URL path, or publishes @option{--data}
and disconnects.
@item pop3, pop3s
Retrieves a message, or lists the messages in the maildrop.
@item scp, sftp
Both run over SFTP on an SSH connection: a file up or down and, for
@code{sftp}, a directory listing.  There is no SCP wire protocol.  SSH
provides the encryption, so there is no TLS variant.  See @ref{SFTP and
SCP}.
@item smtp, smtps
Sends a message to the server, from @option{--mail-from} to each
@option{--mail-rcpt}.
@item telnet
A telnet session: the server's text is written to the output and standard
input is sent.  @xref{Telnet}.
@item tftp
Reads a file, or uploads one with @option{--data} or @option{-T}.  It is
a datagram protocol, so no proxy is used.
@item ws, wss
WebSocket, over HTTP/1, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.  The payload bytes are
written.
@end table

The schemes with a trailing @code{s} (@code{imaps}, @code{ldaps},
@code{mqtts}, @code{pop3s}, @code{smtps}, @code{https}, @code{ftps},
@code{wss}) are TLS from the first byte.  The ones without are not upgraded
unless asked: only @code{ftp}, @code{imap}, @code{pop3} and @code{smtp} can
be, with @option{--ssl} or @option{--ssl-reqd} (@pxref{Explicit TLS}).
@code{ldap} and @code{mqtt} cannot.  A user name and password given for any
of these without the @code{s}, and not upgraded, are sent on a connection that
is not encrypted, and by a method such as @code{PLAIN}, @code{LOGIN} or a
simple bind that does not hide them; @code{PLAIN} is tried when the server
advertises none.  Use the scheme with the @code{s}, or @option{--ssl-reqd}.

@heading Explicit TLS
@anchor{Explicit TLS}

@option{--ssl} and @option{--ssl-reqd} upgrade a connection that starts in the
clear, for @code{ftp}, @code{imap}, @code{pop3} and @code{smtp}, with the
command each protocol has: @code{AUTH TLS} (RFC 4217), @code{STARTTLS}
(RFC 3501 and RFC 3207) and @code{STLS} (RFC 2595).  They do nothing for a
scheme that is TLS already, and nothing for the others.  Without one of them
twist never asks, however much the server offers.

The upgrade comes first.  twist reads what the server offers and asks for
TLS, and the user name and password are sent only after the handshake, which
checks the certificate as for any other TLS connection (@option{--cacert},
@option{-k}).  What the server said before the handshake is forgotten, and its
capabilities are asked for again.  With @code{ftp} the data connection is
then protected as on @code{ftps}, with @code{PBSZ 0} and @code{PROT P}, and a
server that will not ends the transfer.  A server of @code{imap} or
@code{pop3} that has no capability list cannot be asked whether it offers the
upgrade, and counts as not offering it.

@option{--ssl-reqd} ends the transfer with exit status 64 if the server does
not offer the upgrade or refuses it, and nothing, no password, has been sent.
@option{--ssl} goes on in the clear instead.  That is as secure as
@option{--ssl-reqd} when the server agrees and not otherwise, and whoever is on
the path can make a server seem not to offer TLS, so it protects against nobody
who is.  Use @option{--ssl-reqd}.

A server that has said yes and then sends anything before the handshake, in the
same packet or after it, has sent it in the clear, from anyone on the path, and
it is not taken as part of the encrypted session: the transfer ends with exit
status 8.

These schemes are recognised, but are not implemented, and exit 1:
@code{rtmp}, @code{rtmps}, @code{rtsp}, @code{smb} and @code{smbs}.

@heading URLs

A host is a name made of letters, digits and the characters of a name, with a
dot, a hyphen or an underscore, or an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address in
brackets.  A name in UTF-8 is taken.  A space or control character, a
backslash, a percent sign, or one of @samp{| ^ ` < > " @{ @}} in the host, a port
that is not only digits or is above 65535, and anything after the closing
bracket of an IPv6 address other than a port, make a URL malformed: exit status
3.  An empty port, as in @samp{http://host:/}, is the default one.

The path and the query of a request are sent percent-encoded: a space or a byte
above 0x7f is written @samp{%@var{XX}}, and an escape that is there already is
not encoded again.  A request line has no space in it that is not the
separator.

@node Progress meter
@chapter Progress meter

Twist shows the progress of a transfer on standard error, or on
@option{--stderr}: a heading of two lines and then one
line that is written over and over, with the amount transferred, the
speeds, and the time spent and left:

@example
  % Total    % Received % Xferd  Average Speed   Time    Time     Time  Current
                                 Dload  Upload   Total   Spent    Left  Speed
100 80000  100 80000    0     0  40000      0  0:00:02  0:00:02 --:--:-- 40000
@end example

The sizes are in bytes, and with the suffixes k, M, G, T and P as
multiples of 1024.  The columns are the percentage and size of the whole
transfer, the percentage and amount received, the percentage and amount
sent, the average download and upload speeds, the time the transfer will
take, the time it has taken and the time it has left, and the speed
over the last few seconds.  Where a size is not known, the percentages
are 0 and the times @samp{--:--:--}.  The line is drawn when the second
changes, after a second with nothing to draw, and once more at the end.
The end of the line is a newline.

The meter is not shown when the body is written to a terminal, since
it would mix with it: a download, or an upload to HTTP, with no
@option{-o} or @option{-O}, on a terminal.  It is not shown with
@option{-s} or @option{--no-progress-meter}.  @option{-#} draws a bar
instead: a line of @samp{#} signs across the screen and the percentage
when the size is known, and when it is not a spaceship with hash signs
flying above it.  The width is that of the terminal, or the environment
variable @env{COLUMNS} if that is a number from 21 to 9999, and 79 if
neither is known.  The bar ends its line after any error message.

A size is known for the body of an HTTP response that has
Content-Length, a file, an FTP download whose server says how big it is,
an sftp or scp download, an IMAP message, and the data sent with
@option{-d}, @option{-T}, or an SMTP, MQTT, TFTP or sftp upload.  A
redirect finishes the line, and the next response starts again at 0.

@option{-s}, @option{-S}, @option{-#}, @option{--no-progress-meter} and
@option{--stderr} apply to every URL, also after @option{--next}.

@subheading Transfers run together

With @option{-Z} the transfers share one line for the whole run, on standard
error or @option{--stderr}, under a heading that is printed once:

@example
DL% UL%  Dled  Uled  Xfers  Live Total     Current  Left    Speed
 62 --   2500     0     8     3   0:00:01  0:00:01 --:--:--  2500
@end example

The line begins with a carriage return and is written over.  The columns are
the percentage of the download that is done and of the upload; the bytes
downloaded and uploaded; the number of transfers started so far, and of those
that are under way; the time the whole download will take, the time spent and
the time left; and the speed, which is the higher of the download and the
upload over the last ten lines drawn, or since the start before there have
been ten.  Sizes are written as above.  A percentage is shown only when every
transfer under way has a size, and the sizes of all of them, those that are
over too, add up to more than 0; otherwise it is @samp{--}.  A transfer that has
ended stays in the bytes and the sizes.  The times are @samp{--:--:--} when the
download size is not known for every transfer under way, or nothing has moved.
A line is drawn when half a second has passed since the last, and once more
at the end, which ends with a newline.  A message about a transfer that
failed is written after the carriage return, as it comes, and not kept apart
from the line.

The line is not shown with @option{-s} or @option{--no-progress-meter}, nor
when the body of any URL goes to a terminal (a URL with no @option{-o} or
@option{-O}, as above), since it would mix with it.  @option{-#} is ignored:
the line is drawn and not the bar.

A program that uses the library gets the same from
@code{TWISTOPT_NOPROGRESS} (1 by default, which shows nothing),
@code{TWISTOPT_STDERR}, and a callback of its own,
@code{TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION}, which is given the totals and counts
about once a second and when anything changes.  The callback returns 0
to draw nothing, @code{TWIST_PROGRESSFUNC_CONTINUE} to have the library
draw its meter as well, and anything else to stop the transfer with
@code{TWIST_ERR_ABORTED}.  @code{TWISTOPT_INFILESIZE} says how much an
upload with a read function will give.

@node Environment
@chapter Environment

The variables can be in lower case or upper case, and the lower case one
has precedence.  @env{http_proxy} is the exception: it is only read in lower
case, since a web server that runs a program sets the upper case one from the
request.

A proxy from the environment has the same effect as @option{-x}, which
overrides it.  Its value is a proxy URL as @option{-x} takes, with
@code{http://}, @code{https://} or @code{socks5://} before the host, and
@samp{user:password@@} if it wants them.  An empty value is no proxy.

@table @env
@item http_proxy [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTP.
@item HTTPS_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for HTTPS.
@item [url-protocol]_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for the scheme of the URL, whatever it is: @env{FTP_PROXY},
@env{FTPS_PROXY}, @env{SFTP_PROXY}, @env{IMAP_PROXY}, @env{SMTP_PROXY},
@env{LDAP_PROXY} and so on, for the schemes that can go through a proxy.
@env{WS_PROXY} and @env{WSS_PROXY} are those of WebSocket; if they are not set
the proxy of HTTP and HTTPS is used.
@item ALL_PROXY [protocol://]host[:port]
The proxy for a scheme that has none of its own.
@item NO_PROXY comma-separated list of hosts and domains
Hosts that do not go through a proxy.  The list is as for
@option{--noproxy}, which overrides it: a name matches itself and the names
under it, an address matches itself or a network written as
@samp{192.168.0.0/16}, an IPv6 address is written without brackets, and a
single @samp{*} matches all hosts.  This disables the proxy also when it was
given with @option{-x}.
@item COLUMNS terminal width
If it is a number from 21 to 9999, that many characters are the width of the
bar of @option{-#}.  Without it the width of the terminal is used, and 79 if
there is none.
@item HOME directory
Where @file{.ssh/known_hosts} is looked for, for sftp and scp.  If it is not
set, the home directory in the password file.
@item QLOGDIR directory name
Makes twist write a log of each QUIC connection (HTTP/3 and DNS over QUIC) in
that directory, in a file named after the connection ID the client chose, in
hexadecimal, and the ending @file{.qlog}.  Each line is an event of the
connection, as draft-ietf-quic-qlog-quic-events defines them, with the time in
microseconds, its name and its fields as @samp{KEY=VALUE}.  The file can be read
by the owner only.  A directory that is not there means no log.  These files
can become large, and say who was talked to.
@item SSH_AUTH_SOCK file name
The socket of the ssh-agent that holds the keys for sftp and scp, as for ssh.
@item SSL_CERT_DIR directory
If set, it is used as the @option{--capath} value.
@item SSL_CERT_FILE file name
If set, it is used as the @option{--cacert} value.
@item SSLKEYLOGFILE file name
If you set this to a file name, twist writes the secrets of its TLS
connections in that file, in the NSS key log format, so that Wireshark and
similar tools can decrypt a capture of them.  This includes HTTP/3.  The file
is made so that only its owner can read it, and what is in it undoes the
encryption of the connections: it is for debugging.
@item TWIST_CA_BUNDLE file name
If set, it is used as the @option{--cacert} value, and @env{SSL_CERT_DIR} and
@env{SSL_CERT_FILE} are not looked at.
@end table

The names of the certificate variables are looked at only when neither
@option{--cacert} nor @option{--capath} is given.  A file or directory that
they name and that cannot be read is an error.

A process that has more rights than the user who started it does not read the
environment at all, for any of the variables above that name a file, a
directory, a socket or a server: @env{HOME}, the proxy variables and
@env{NO_PROXY}, @env{QLOGDIR}, @env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}, @env{SSL_CERT_DIR},
@env{SSL_CERT_FILE}, @env{SSLKEYLOGFILE} and @env{TWIST_CA_BUNDLE}.  That is
a program that is set-user-ID or set-group-ID, one whose real and effective
IDs differ, and one that the kernel started with more rights (file
capabilities).  Whoever can set a variable for such a program can otherwise
send it to a proxy of their choosing, make it trust their certificates, or
have it write its keys to a file.  Options still work.

Twist reads no configuration file.  Variables can also be imported by @option{--variable %@var{name}} (@pxref{Variables}).

@node Variables
@chapter Variables

A command line can set variables and use them in the parameters of options.

@example
twist --variable name=smith --expand-url 'https://example.com/@{@{name@}@}' ...
twist --variable who=smith --expand-data '@{"user": "@{@{who:json@}@}"@}' ...
twist --variable '%HOME' --expand-output '@{@{HOME@}@}/page.html' URL
@end example

@option{--variable name=text} gives @var{name} the text; @option{--variable
name@@file} gives it the contents of the file, and @samp{-} for the file is
standard input.  A name is letters, digits and underscores, and case matters;
the content can be anything, to ten megabytes.  A variable that is set again
has its new content.  The environment is imported by name: @option{--variable
%name} takes it from there and is an error if the environment does not have it;
@option{--variable %name=text} (or @samp{%name@@file}) says what it is when the
environment does not, and the environment's value is used when it does, even if
that is empty.

A variable is used in the parameter of an option whose name has @samp{expand-}
put before it.  @option{--expand-data} is @option{--data} with each
@samp{@{@{name@}@}} in the parameter replaced by the content.  The option
may be given as @option{--expand-url=value} too.  This works for every long
option that takes a parameter, and not for a URL given as an argument, which
is not an option: use @option{--url} for that.  A variable is what it is at the
place on the command line where it is used, so it is set before it is needed;
variables stay across @option{--next}.

@itemize
@item
A name that is not set is empty.
@item
@samp{\@{@{} is @samp{@{@{} and is not the start of a variable.
@item
Anything that is not @samp{@{@{name@}@}} (with a name of the right kind, and no
spaces) is left as it is.
@item
The content is not expanded again.
@item
The expanded text is a string, so content with a NUL in it is an error unless
a function encodes it (@code{b64}, @code{json} or @code{url}).
@item
The result may not be over ten megabytes.
@end itemize

Functions follow the name after colons, and are applied from left to right:
@samp{@{@{name:trim:b64@}@}}.  A comma can separate them too.

@table @code
@item trim
Takes the white space off both ends.
@item json
Writes the content as the inside of a JSON string: quotes, backslashes and
control characters escaped, UTF-8 left as it is.  The quotes are not added.
@item url
Percent-encodes everything but letters, digits and @samp{-._~}.
@item b64
Base64.
@item 64dec
From base64, padded or not.  What is not base64 gives @samp{[64dec-fail]}.
@end table

An error in a variable or an expansion is a bad option: the message names the
option and what is wrong, and the status is 2 (26 for a file that cannot be
read).  A URL that comes out of an expansion is globbed afterwards
(@pxref{URL globbing}), so content with braces or brackets in it, put in a URL,
should go through @code{url} or be used with @option{-g}.

@node URL globbing
@chapter URL globbing

A URL may name many things at once, for every scheme:

@table @code
@item @{a,b,c@}
One of these, in turn.  A set cannot hold another set or a range, and an
empty element is the empty string.  In a set a backslash makes the character
after it plain.
@item [1-10]
A range of numbers.  @samp{[01-10]} keeps the width of its start, with
zeros, and @samp{[1-10:3]} takes every third.
@item [a-z]
A range of letters of one case, which also takes a step.
@end table

A URL with several makes all the combinations, in order, the last going
fastest: @samp{@{x,y@}/[1-2]} is @samp{x/1}, @samp{x/2}, @samp{y/1},
@samp{y/2}.  They are made one at a time, so a range of billions starts at
once.  Outside a set a backslash before a brace or bracket makes it plain, and
the brackets of an IPv6 address are not a range.  A pattern that is wrong is a
malformed URL (status 3) and the message shows the place:

@example
twist: (3) bad range in URL position 25:
http://127.0.0.1:1/[3-1]
                        ^
@end example

The name given to @option{-o} may hold @samp{#1}, @samp{#2} and so on, for
what the first glob, the second, and so on, were in the URL that is being
fetched: @samp{twist -o 'page_#1.html' 'https://example.com/p[1-3].html'}
writes @file{page_1.html} to @file{page_3.html}.  A number with no glob is
text, as is a @samp{#} with no number; a backslash does not stop it.  The
@option{-o} goes with the URL it is given next to, and so with everything that
URL makes: a name with no @samp{#} is written by each in turn.  @option{-O}
names each file from its own URL.  Each transfer is a transfer: a failure of
one does not stop the rest unless @option{--fail-early} says so, and the
status is the last one's (with @option{-Z}, the most recent failure).  @option{-g} turns it all off.

Over @code{sftp} the URLs for one server share a connection, so
@samp{sftp://host/dir/f[1-100].txt} logs in once.  There is no wildcard
in a remote path, such as @samp{*.txt}.

@node SFTP and SCP
@chapter SFTP and SCP

@example
twist sftp://user@@host/path/to/file
twist scp://user@@host/~/file
@end example

twist transfers a file over SSH for each URL, up or down, and lists a directory
for an @code{sftp} URL.  Several URLs on a command line are several transfers,
and those for one server share a connection (see @emph{Several URLs} below).  URLs are
expanded as any URL is (@pxref{URL globbing}) with @samp{@{a,b@}} and
@samp{[1-3]}, but a path has no wildcard such as @samp{*}.  @code{scp://} and @code{sftp://} are the same transfer, which uses the SFTP subsystem of the server: twist does not
implement the old SCP protocol, as OpenSSH's own @command{scp} has not since
version 9.0.  A server with no SFTP subsystem fails with an error that says
so.  The path is absolute, except that @samp{/~/} begins in the directory
the server puts the user in.  An @code{scp} URL does not expand wildcards.

@subheading Directory listings

An @code{sftp} URL that ends in @samp{/}, or has no path, lists that
directory: @samp{sftp://host/} the root, @samp{sftp://host/~/} the user's home
directory, @samp{sftp://host/dir/} a directory.  Each entry is a line, in
the long form the server gives (like @command{ls -l}), in the order the server
sends them, including @file{.}, @file{..} and files whose names begin with a
dot.  @option{-l} gives the names only.

What a server sends is not trusted with the output: a newline in a file name
would make a line that is not an entry, and control characters can do as they
please to a terminal.  So control characters in a listing, and the UTF-8 form
of the C1 ones, are replaced with @samp{?}. 

An @code{scp} URL does not list: it names one file, and a URL that ends in
@samp{/} exits 78.  A directory that is not there, or a file followed by a
slash, exits 78 too, and one that may not be listed exits 9.  A range or
@option{-C} means nothing for a listing and is refused.

@subheading Upload, resume and ranges

@option{-T @var{file}} uploads a file.  The remote file is created, with mode
0644, and truncated if it is there.  When the URL names a
directory (it ends in @samp{/}) the file gets the name of the local file.
Data given with @option{-d} is uploaded the same way.  If the
file cannot be read part way, the transfer fails with status 26; the remote
file is left as far as it got.  A pipe is read as it delivers
data, and twist does not do anything else while it waits for the next of it.

@option{-C} continues a transfer that stopped.  An upload does not send what
the server has, and writes on at that offset of the remote file without
truncating it.  A download is added to the end of the output file.

@option{-r} downloads part of a file: @samp{100-199} (both ends included),
@samp{100-} (to the end), or @samp{-50} (the last 50 bytes).  A range that
starts beyond the end of the file exits 33.  A range and @option{-C} cannot
be used together.

The size of the remote file is asked for only when it is needed: to check a
download resume, to find the start of @samp{-50}, or to resume an upload.
A server that does not say how big a file is cannot do the last, nor
@samp{-50}.

@subheading Time limits

@option{--connect-timeout} is one limit for all of making the connection: the
name lookup, the TCP connect, a proxy's tunnel, the SSH banner, the key
exchange and the login.  A server or a proxy that takes the connection and does
not go on is given up on with status 28, where it would otherwise be waited for.
It ends at the login: a transfer that takes longer than the limit is not cut
off by it, and a session that is kept for the next URL is not connecting at all.
The key exchange gets the same time, not a limit of its own.
@option{--max-time} limits the whole, as before.

@subheading Through a proxy

@option{-x} opens a tunnel to the SSH server through an HTTP proxy
(@code{CONNECT}) or a SOCKS5 proxy, and the SSH session goes through it.
Credentials in the proxy's URL, or given with @option{-U}, are sent as Basic
for HTTP and as a user name and password for SOCKS5.  A refusal is told
with a status: 56 for a @code{CONNECT} that failed
(@samp{CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403}), 97 for SOCKS5 (@samp{Can't
complete SOCKS5 connection to host. (5)}); a proxy that is not there is 7.
The server's banner may arrive in the same packet as the proxy's reply, and
a proxy may say a good deal before it opens the tunnel; both are handled.

The server is named to the proxy as the URL gives it: a name is sent as a name,
and an IP address as an address (an IPv6 one in brackets for @code{CONNECT}).
@code{socks5://} and @code{socks5h://} are the same: twist does not look the
name up itself before giving it to a SOCKS5 proxy.  The tunnel is kept for the next URL as a connection is, but
only for the same proxy with the same credentials: a login made through one
proxy is not used by a transfer through another, or through none.

@subheading IPv6 addresses

An IPv6 address is written in brackets, @samp{sftp://user@@[2001:db8::7]:2222/f}.
known_hosts names it as @command{ssh} does: bare for port 22
(@samp{2001:db8::7}), and in brackets with the port for any other
(@samp{[2001:db8::7]:2222}).  A link-local address names the interface it is
reached by after a percent sign, which a URL writes encoded:
@samp{[fe80::1%25eth0]} (a bare @samp{%eth0} is taken too).  known_hosts keeps
the zone, @samp{fe80::1%eth0}, as @command{ssh} does, and the connection goes
out by that interface; without it a link-local address goes nowhere.  An
interface may be given by number as well.  What is between brackets must be
an IPv6 address: anything else is a malformed URL (status 3), and an interface
that this machine does not have is a connection that cannot be made (status
7).  Neither waits for a lookup.  The effective URL (@samp{%@{url_effective@}})
keeps the brackets and the zone, and an HTTP request names the host in its
@code{Host} header with the brackets and without the zone.

@subheading Commands and missing directories

@option{-Q @var{command}} runs a command on the server before the transfer,
and @option{-Q -@var{command}} after it, if it succeeded.  An @code{sftp} URL
takes them; an @code{scp} URL, like any other kind, ignores them.  They run
one at a time, in the order given, and the first the server refuses ends the
transfer with status 21 and a message that says which command and why.  A
@samp{*} before a command (@samp{-Q '*rm old'}) lets the server refuse it.  A
command twist cannot read is an error even then.  A @samp{+} before a command
is refused: it is the form for FTP.

The command is the word up to
the first space, in any case; a name may be in double quotes to hold spaces,
with @samp{\"} and @samp{\\} for a quote and a backslash in it.  Single quotes
mean nothing.  A relative name is the server's to place, in the user's
directory.

@table @code
@item chmod @var{mode} @var{file}
The mode is octal.
@item chown @var{uid} @var{file}
@itemx chgrp @var{gid} @var{file}
A number.  The other of the two is kept as it is.
@item mtime @var{date} @var{file}
@itemx atime @var{date} @var{file}
The date is one parameter, so in quotes: @samp{"Thu, 02 Jan 2020 10:00:00
GMT"}, with or without the day of the week, a zone as @samp{GMT} or
@samp{+0100} (none: local time), or as @code{asctime} writes it.  The other
time is kept as it is.
@item ln @var{source} @var{link}
@itemx symlink @var{source} @var{link}
Make @var{link} a symbolic link to @var{source}.
@item mkdir @var{dir}
@itemx rmdir @var{dir}
@itemx rm @var{file}
@itemx rename @var{from} @var{to}
As the names say.  @code{mkdir} makes a directory with mode 0755.
@item pwd
The server's current directory (its home), written to the header output
(@option{-D}, @option{-i}) as @samp{257 "/home/me" is current directory.}
@end table

@option{-I} runs the commands and looks at the file, and transfers nothing:
status 78 if it is not there.  That is a way to run commands alone.
@option{--ftp-create-dirs} makes the directories of the path of an upload
that are not there, from the top, and tries again; a directory that is there
is left, and if making them does not help the error is the upload's.

@subheading Rate limits

@option{--limit-rate} limits the average over the transfer.
A chunk is sent or asked for when the bytes before it have been earned at the
limit, so the transfer is ahead by at most one chunk.  A chunk is 32 KiB, or
half a second's worth of the limit when that is less, so a slow limit gives
small requests and not lumps; at 1000 bytes a second the requests are of 500
bytes.  The time spent waiting for a slow disk or a slow pipe is time, so
it counts.  The library takes @code{TWISTOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE} and
@code{TWISTOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE} for the two directions.

@subheading Logging in

A user name or password written in the URL is percent-encoded, as it must be
if it holds an @samp{@@}, a @samp{:} or a @samp{/}: @samp{p@@ss} is
@samp{p%40ss}.  twist uses what the encoding says.  This holds for every
scheme.  A name or password that decodes to a carriage return, a line feed or
a NUL is refused as a malformed URL, since it would otherwise reach a command
the protocol is built from.

Keys are tried in this order.  A key named with @option{--key} is used alone,
and nothing else is asked.  Otherwise the keys in an @command{ssh-agent}, in
the agent's order, then @file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519}, @file{id_ecdsa} and
@file{id_rsa}.  Then a password, from @option{-u} or the URL.

A server may ask for the password as a question (keyboard-interactive login,
usual with PAM) and not as a password.  twist answers when the server asks
one question that is not shown as it is typed, and the answer is the
password.  Anything else, such as a verification code or
several questions, is answered with nothing, so the password is not given to
a question that might not be asking for it; the other ways are then tried, and
if they fail too the message says what was asked.  With no password, the
method is not tried.  A server that offers both a password and questions is
asked the question first, so a wrong password can count as two failed
attempts, as it does for @command{ssh}.

A key may have an OpenSSH user certificate, as @command{ssh} finds it: the
file of the key with @file{-cert.pub} added (@file{~/.ssh/id_ed25519-cert.pub},
or @file{key-cert.pub} for @option{--key key}).  The certificate is offered
before the key, which is still offered after it.  A certificate that cannot
be used is not offered, because the server only says that it refused: if the
login then fails the message says that the certificate has expired, is not yet
good, is not for the key, or does not name the user.  Certificates in an agent
need nothing from twist: @command{ssh-add} loads them with the key.  twist
does not check who signed a certificate; the server decides which CAs it
trusts.

The agent is the one named by the environment variable @env{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}.
twist asks it what it holds and to sign; the private keys never reach twist.
This includes keys that are protected by a passphrase, restricted to certain
servers (@command{ssh-add -h}), or kept on a security key.  Whatever is wrong
with the agent, whether there is none, it does not answer, or it refuses, is
not fatal: the other keys and the password are tried, and if they all fail
the message says what the agent did.  A server that counts every key it turns
away as a failed attempt, as @command{sshd} does, allows a limited number of
keys in the agent to be tried, six unless it is set otherwise.  This is the
server's limit, as it is for @command{ssh}.

The key files twist reads are OpenSSH's, PEM and PKCS#8, and PuTTY's version
3.  @strong{A passphrase-protected key file in the OpenSSH format, which is
what @command{ssh-keygen} writes by default, cannot be read}, because its key
derivation is not in libgcrypt and twist does not take it from anywhere else.
Put the key in an agent (@command{ssh-add}), which can use it, or convert it
with @command{ssh-keygen -p -m PKCS8} and give its passphrase with
@option{--pass}.

The library takes the agent's socket from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_AGENT_SOCKET}; an
empty string means no agent.

@subheading The host key

The host key is checked against @file{~/.ssh/known_hosts}.  Here, and for the key files above, @file{~} is @env{HOME}, or the home
directory in the password file if @env{HOME} is not set or is empty.  A host that is not in the file, or whose key differs from the one in
it, fails the transfer (status 60); there is no way to accept a new host
key by asking.  Add it with @command{ssh-keyscan}, connect once with
@command{ssh}, or give its fingerprint with @option{--hostpubsha256}.
@option{-k} turns the check off.  A host known by one kind of key is asked
for that kind, so it cannot answer with another.

The library takes the same from @code{TWISTOPT_SSH_KNOWNHOSTS},
@code{TWISTOPT_SSH_HOST_PUBLIC_KEY_SHA256}, @code{TWISTOPT_SSLKEY} and
@code{TWISTOPT_KEYPASSWD}.

@subheading Several URLs

URLs for the same server on one command line are fetched on one connection:
the key exchange and the login happen once, and the SFTP channel stays open.
@code{sftp://} and @code{scp://} share it.  A connection is used again only
for the same host, port and user, and only when everything that decides who
you are or whom twist trusts is the same: the password, the key and its
passphrase, the known_hosts file, a pinned fingerprint, the agent, and whether
the host key is checked at all.  A session that was made with @option{-k}
is never used for a transfer that checks the host key.  A transfer that
fails closes its connection, and the next URL logs in again.  A connection
that is idle for two minutes, or that the server closes, is dropped.

With the library, the connections are kept in the runtime that the transfers
run on, for the next transfer started on it.

@subheading Requirements

SSH comes from the foxtrot library, which needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later.

@node Telnet
@chapter Telnet

@code{telnet://host[:port]/} (port 23) opens a connection and runs a telnet
session on it (RFC 854): what the server sends is written to the output, and
what is read from standard input is sent to the server.  The path of the URL is
not used.  The session ends when the server closes the connection, with exit
status 0, or at the limit of @option{-m}.

@subheading What is sent and what is written

What is sent is standard input, which is read as it comes, so that a terminal,
a pipe or a file may be used and what is typed goes to the server at once; or
the file of @option{-T}, and @option{-T -} is standard input.  Each byte of 255
is sent twice, as the protocol needs.  Nothing else is added or changed: a line
ends as it does in the input.  The data of @option{-d} is not sent.  The end of
the input does not end the session, which is the server's to end; a server that
does not close is waited for until @option{-m}.

What is written is the server's text without its commands.  A carriage return
followed by a NUL is a carriage return, and 255 twice is a byte of 255.  A
command that twist does not know is dropped.  A transfer that is much behind the
connection waits for it, and does not hold the input in memory.

@subheading Negotiation

twist negotiates with a server that negotiates, and only then.  It speaks
plain text to a server that does not, so that @code{telnet://} may be used to
talk to a mail or an echo service.  The first time the server sends a command
(WILL, WON'T, DO or DON'T), twist answers it and asks for the options it wants:
binary transmission and suppress go-ahead, both ways.  It accepts the server's
echo, but does not ask for it.  Every other option the server offers or asks for
is refused, with these exceptions, which are given what they ask for:

@table @asis
@item terminal type
@itemx X display location
@itemx environment variables
A server that asks, with a sub-negotiation, is told the values of the
@code{TTYPE}, @code{XDISPLOC} and @code{NEW_ENV} options, or nothing for
those that were not given.  Giving one of the options also offers it, with
the other options twist wants, when the negotiation starts.
@item window size
A server that asks is told the size of @code{WS}, or 0 by 0 if it was not
given, which is allowed and means that the server may guess.
@end table

@subheading The options

@option{-t} (@code{TWISTOPT_TELNETOPTIONS}) takes @var{name}=@var{value}, with
the name in either case:

@table @code
@item TTYPE=@var{type}
The terminal type, such as @code{vt100}, up to 31 bytes.
@item XDISPLOC=@var{display}
The X display, such as @code{host:0}, up to 127 bytes.
@item NEW_ENV=@var{name},@var{value}
An environment variable.  Give the option once for each.  @code{NEW_ENV=name}
is a variable with no value.
@item WS=@var{width}x@var{height}
The size of the window in characters, each from 1 to 65535.
@item BINARY=@var{n}
1, the default, asks for binary transmission; any other number does not.
@end table

The user name of @option{-u} or of the URL is sent as the variable @code{USER},
and so offers the environment to the server.  A value with a byte above 127 is
skipped without an error; a user name with one is refused (exit status 43).  A
name that is not one of these is exit status 48, and an option with no
@samp{=} or with a bad value is exit status 49.  Both are found before twist
connects.

@subheading Not done

There is no TLS variant, @option{--limit-rate} is refused, and the size of a
terminal is not found out and sent: use @code{WS}.  A proxy can be used, and the
session goes through its tunnel.

